00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:05.930 align:start position:0%
European Union transport ministers are  discussing ways to increase the  availability of alternative fuels across  the EU to help stimulate demand for  clean power the aim is to reduce  Europe's dependence on fossil fuels  improve energy security and cut  greenhouse gas emissions from sources  like Motor Vehicles the proposal  includes issues like the minimum number  of recharge points for electric cars in  EU countries or requirements for natural  gas and hydrogen refueling stations in  member states where such refueling  possibilities exist in cities the idea  would be to encourage more to be built  in outlying areas so that clean vehicles  can be used throughout the country  putting in place sufficient  infrastructure and setting common  standards across Europe are considered  crucial to boosting market demand for  alternative fuels at the same time the  move would stimulate demand plus  research and development in consultation  with Member States the European  Commission hopes to publish in the near  future national targets for this clean  energy infrastructure with the aim of  having it all in place by 2030
 

